// HTTP/2 crawler defense on the decoded headers. Over HTTP/2 a User-Agent never
// appears as text on the wire: it is Huffman-coded the first time and sent as a
// one-byte dynamic-table index after that, so a byte scan of the packets sees
// nothing. `decode h2` reassembles the stream, parses the frames and HPACK-decodes
// every completed header block (Huffman and the per-connection dynamic table
// included) into "name: value\n" lines, and the program matches those.
//
// The match is anchored to the header: `within(agent, 256)` reports only what
// lies in the 256 bytes after "user-agent: ", so a path such as
// /blog/gptbot-news does not trip it. (A plain second `scan` would not do: the
// match mask accumulates across scans, and the first one already saw the path.
// 256 bytes can run past the value into the following header lines.)
//
// Plaintext HTTP/2 only (h2c, e.g. the hop behind a TLS terminator or a gRPC
// mesh): TLS-wrapped h2 on :443 is ciphertext at XDP. HTTP/1.1 arriving on the
// same port is matched as a plain stream, where the same anchor works.
//
// Combines: the h2 decode stage (implies reassembly), a cursor anchor (after),
// any() across several patterns, and a source ban.
nocase;
decode h2 8080;
pattern ua = "user-agent: ";
pattern gptbot = "GPTBot", claudebot = "ClaudeBot", bytespider = "Bytespider";
filter {
  if (len == 0) decide pass;                  // nothing decoded in this packet
  if (!(scan(payload, 4096) & ua)) decide pass;
  ptr agent = after(ua);                      // the User-Agent value
  if (any(within(agent, 256), gptbot, claudebot, bytespider)) decide ban("h2_crawler", 300);
  decide pass;
}
